==Trivia==
* There is at least one Mega-Evolved {{OBP|Pokémon|species}} from every [[generation]] and of every [[type]].
** Currently, [[Generation III]] has both the most Mega Evolutions and the most Pokémon capable of Mega Evolving (with 22 and 21, respectively), while [[Generation VIII]] has the fewest overall (with {{p|Falinks|one}}).
** {{t|Dragon}} has the most Mega Evolutions (with 17), while {{t|Rock}} and {{t|Ice}} are tied for having the fewest (with five each).
*** Dragon is also the most common type gained by Mega Pokémon, with four.
** No Mega-Evolved Pokémon has {{t|Flying}} as its primary type.
* Only fully evolved Pokémon are capable of Mega Evolution.
** {{p|Floette}} is the only exception to this, though only in its {{form|Floette|Eternal Flower}} [[form]], which is incapable of evolving into or from any Pokémon.
* {{p|Charizard}}, {{p|Raichu}}, {{p|Mewtwo}}, {{p|Absol}}, {{p|Lucario}}, and {{p|Garchomp}} all have more than one Mega Evolution, accessible through distinct [[Mega Stone]]s.
** Similarly, {{p|Magearna}} and {{p|Tatsugiri}} have different appearances while Mega-Evolved depending on their form (Magearna's {{form|Magearna|regular and Original Color forms}} and Tatsugiri's {{form|Tatsugiri|Curly, Droopy, and Stretchy Forms}}), but are nonetheless considered to have only one Mega Evolution each.
* All Pokémon that evolve with a [[Dawn Stone]] or [[trade]]d while holding a [[Metal Coat]] are capable of Mega Evolution.
* In regards to [[type]]s:
** 74 Pokémon retain their original type combination upon Mega Evolving.
** {{p|Clefable}}, {{p|Pinsir}}, Mewtwo (into Mega Mewtwo X), {{p|Meganium}}, {{p|Feraligatr}}, {{p|Ampharos}}, {{p|Sceptile}}, {{p|Chimecho}}, Absol (into Mega Absol Z), {{p|Lopunny}}, and {{p|Audino}} all become dual-type upon Mega Evolving.
** {{p|Aggron}} and Garchomp (into Mega Garchomp Z) both become single-type upon Mega Evolving.
** Charizard (into Mega Charizard X), {{p|Gyarados}}, {{p|Altaria}}, {{p|Staraptor}}, {{p|Barbaracle}}, and {{p|Golisopod}} all change one of their types to a new one upon Mega Evolving.
*** Notably, Staraptor is the only such case in which it changes its primary type, rather than its secondary one.
* Mega Feraligatr, Mega Audino, and Mega Golisopod are the only Mega Evolutions that do not share any [[weakness]]es with their normal forms.
* As of [[Generation IX]], there are ten {{Abilities}} {{cat|Mega Evolution-exclusive Abilities|exclusive}} to Mega-Evolved Pokémon.
* In regards to Abilities:
** 20 Pokémon retain one of their possible Abilities upon Mega Evolution.
*** Out of them, {{p|Latios}}, {{p|Latias}}, and Chimecho are the only ones who share the same Ability in {{a|Levitate}}.
** Intimidate is the most common Ability shared by Pokémon capable of Mega Evolution, with five Pokémon having it as a possible Ability.
** Levitate and {{a|Tough Claws}} are the most common Abilities among Mega-Evolved Pokémon, each with five Pokémon.
** Tough Claws is the most common Ability to be gained upon Mega Evolution, with four Pokémon having their Ability changed into it.
* Some Pokémon capable of Mega Evolution also have access to other forms or {{pkmn|battle}} mechanics:
** Raichu and {{p|Slowbro}} are the only Pokémon to have a Mega Evolution and a [[regional form]].
** Raichu, between its [[Kanto]]nian and [[Alolan form]], is the only Pokémon to have Mega Evolutions and an exclusive [[Z-Move]], {{m|Stoked Sparksurfer}}.
** The fully evolved [[Kanto first partner Pokémon]] and {{p|Gengar}} are the only Pokémon that can both Mega Evolve and [[Gigantamax]].
* Some Mega-Evolved Pokémon have unique attributes:
** Mega Altaria is the only one with a [[List of Pokémon with unique type combinations|unique type combination]].
** Mega {{p|Zygarde}} is the only one whose moveset changes upon Mega Evolving, with {{m|Nihil Light}} replacing {{m|Core Enforcer}}.
** Mega {{p|Eelektross}} is the only one with no type weaknesses under normal battle conditions.
* {{p|Aerodactyl}} is the only [[Fossil|Fossil Pokémon]] capable of Mega Evolving.
* Rayquaza is the only Pokémon to not have a designated Mega Stone, as it doesn't need one to Mega Evolve in the core series games that include Mega Evolution, instead being required to know {{m|Dragon Ascent}}.
** This makes Rayquaza the only Pokémon that can have a different held item while Mega Evolving, since Mega Stones cannot be taken off their proper holder during battle.
** It is, furthermore, the only Pokémon to lack the message "(The opposing) <Pokémon>'s  is reacting to its [[Key Stone]]!" when it Mega Evolves, as it has the message "'s fervent wish has reached Rayquaza!" instead.
** In [[Pokémon Shuffle]], Rayquaza needs the player to get the [[Meteorite]] for it to Mega Evolve.

===Designs===
* Design-wise, multiple Mega-Evolved Pokémon seem to incorporate traits from their previous [[evolution]]ary stages:
** Mega {{p|Venusaur}} restores patches of dark teal spots on its face similar to those of {{p|Bulbasaur}} and {{p|Ivysaur}} around their bodies.
** Continuing the design traits that both {{p|Charmeleon}} and {{p|Charizard}} had, as well as some elements of {{p|Charmander}} before its [[Generation III]] redesign, Mega Charizard Y features numerous spikes and horns along its neck and tail.
** Both of {{p|Raichu}}'s Mega Evolutions have {{p|Pikachu}}-like features:<ref>[https://legends.pokemon.com/en-gb/news/mega-dimension-hoopa The Mythical Pokémon Hoopa Holds the Key to the Story | Pokémon Legends: Z-A]</ref> Mega Raichu X has red cheeks, bright yellow fur, and a forked zig-zag tail, while Mega Raichu Y has Pikachu's body shape, ears, and tail.
** Mega {{p|Clefable}} has dark pink, oval cheek markings resembling those of {{p|Cleffa}} and {{p|Clefairy}}.
** Mega {{p|Dragonite}} gains features distinctive of {{p|Dragonair}}, such as the wings on its head and the pearl on its tail.<ref>[https://legends.pokemon.com/en-us/story-world/pokemon/mega-dragonite Mega Dragonite | Pokémon Legends: Z-A]</ref> Furthermore, its {{Shiny}} is azure-colored, which calls back to {{p|Dratini}} and Dragonair's regular colorations.
** Mega {{p|Feraligatr}}'s dorsal fin resembles a gigantic {{p|Totodile}}'s head, leading to it being referred to as a "Totodile mask".<ref>[https://legends.pokemon.com/en-us/story-world/pokemon/mega-feraligatr Mega Totodile | Pokémon Legends: Z-A]</ref>
** Mega {{p|Ampharos}}'s tail and head are covered in white fur,<ref>[http://www.pokemonxy.com/en-us/pokemon/mega_pokemon/#Ampharos Mega Ampharos | Pokémon X and Pokémon Y] ([https://web.archive.org/web/20150427074403/http://www.pokemonxy.com/en-us/pokemon/mega_pokemon/#Ampharos archived])</ref> which is the wool it originally had as a {{p|Mareep}} and {{p|Flaaffy}} that has regrown due to the excess energy from Mega Evolution stimulating its genes.
** Like its base form, Mega {{p|Tyranitar}}'s chest plate, back spikes, and eye-like marking resemble {{p|Pupitar}}. The horn atop its head similarly resembles {{p|Larvitar}}'s own, and the red eyes and diamonds reference Larvitar as well.
** Mega {{p|Aggron}}'s head plate is reminiscent of {{p|Lairon}}'s in reverse, while base form Aggron's is reminiscent of {{p|Aron}}'s.
** Mega {{p|Chimecho}} grows a golden frame with fake eyes and arms reminiscent of {{p|Chingling}}.
** Mega {{p|Salamence}} has shorter forelimbs and longer hind limbs than its base form, as well as a smooth lower jaw, all of which bring to mind {{p|Bagon}}. The armor encasing its body and substantial Defense increase bear homage to {{p|Shelgon}}; its green sclera likewise evoke Shelgon's yellow sclera.
** Mega {{p|Metagross}} is said to be the result of one Metagross, one {{p|Metang}}, and two {{p|Beldum}} linking up. Its body plan strongly resembles Metang's as well.
** Mega {{p|Scolipede}}'s lavender-colored armor is reminiscent of {{p|Whirlipede}}; this holds true for the violet armor of its Shiny coloration.
** Mega {{p|Eelektross}}'s arms are covered by mucus in the shape of {{p|Eelektrik}}, named "false Eelektrik".<ref>[https://legends.pokemon.com/en-us/story-world/pokemon/mega-eelektross Mega Eelektross | Pokémon Legends: Z-A]</ref>
** Mega {{p|Glimmora}}'s main body resembles {{p|Glimmet}}.
* Mega {{p|Rayquaza}} features a {{wp|Delta (letter)|delta}} motif, matching the designs of [[Primal Reversion|Primal]] {{p|Kyogre}} and {{p|Groudon}} (which incorporate the Greek letters {{wp|alpha}} and {{wp|omega}}, respectively). As the letter delta resembles a triangle when capitalized, it may also connect to {{p|Deoxys}}, which is known to assume a triangle-shaped form when inactive.
* No Mega-Evolved Pokémon has [[List of Pokémon with gender differences|gender differences]], even if they Mega Evolve from a Pokémon that has one.
** For Pokémon with significant differences due to gender, the Mega-Evolved form is a combination of the male and female forms: Mega {{p|Pyroar}} combines the male and female forms' respective manes into one, while Mega {{p|Meowstic}} resembles a {{wp|Chimera (genetics)|chimeric}} fusion of the male and female forms.
** While technically different species, Mega {{p|Latias}} and Mega {{p|Latios}} resemble one another much more strongly than their base forms. Additionally, Latias grows more than Latios when Mega Evolving, which brings them closer together in height and weight.
* In contrast to the other [[Paradox Pokémon]], {{p|Roaring Moon}} and {{p|Iron Valiant}}'s designs have elements of Mega Salamence and Mega {{p|Gardevoir}} and {{p|Gallade}} instead of their regular forms.
** Roaring Moon has been compared specifically to Salamence "when they undergo a certain phenomenon" (i.e. Mega Salamence) in a copy of {{DL|Magazines in the Pokémon world|Occulture}} magazine in [[Pokémon Scarlet and Violet|Pokémon Scarlet]], though it is left uncertain if there is any connection between them. Roaring Moon's [[Pokédex entry]] in [[Pokémon Scarlet and Violet|Pokémon Violet]] references the Occulture article and Mega Evolution as well.
** An article on the English Press Website for [[The Pokémon Company International]] on Pokémon Legends: Z-A's March 2025 [[Nintendo Direct]] trailer accidentally labeled one of the files corresponding to Mega Salamence as "Roaring Moon". This was fixed later that day.<ref>[https://press.pokemon.com/en/releases/New-Pokemon-Legends-Z-A-Details-Revealed-During-Nintendo-Direct The Pokémon Company International Official Press Site: New Pokémon Legends: Z-A Details Revealed During Nintendo Direct]</ref><ref>([https://web.archive.org/web/20250327154906/https://press.pokemon.com/en/releases/New-Pokemon-Legends-Z-A-Details-Revealed-During-Nintendo-Direct archived])</ref>

===Core series===
* The Japanese and Korean logos for {{g|X and Y}} include the Mega Evolution sigil.
* At least one Mega Evolution of every type was introduced in each core series game that introduced new Mega Evolutions, with the exception of the {{t|Electric}} type in {{g|Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire}}.
** If counted on its own, the [[Mega Dimension]] [[Downloadable content|DLC]] for {{g|Legends: Z-A}} also did not introduce a Mega Evolution of each type, with no new Mega-Evolved Pokémon being {{t|Normal}} type. However, counting {{p|Staraptor}} (who loses its {{type|Normal}} typing upon Mega Evolving) at least one Pokémon of every type is given a new Mega Evolution.
* Due to Mega Evolution being absent from {{g|Sword and Shield}} through {{g|Scarlet and Violet}}, the status of some Abilities was affected:
** {{a|Aerilate}}, {{a|Parental Bond}}, and {{a|Delta Stream}} became unusable despite existing in the game data.
** {{a|Refrigerate}}, {{a|Mega Launcher}}, and {{a|Pixilate}} became the signature Ability of {{p|Amaura}}'s evolutionary line, {{p|Clauncher}}'s evolutionary line, and {{p|Sylveon}}, respectively.
* Mega {{p|Latias}} and Mega {{p|Latios}} are programmed into Pokémon X and Y; as a result, they are not banned by the Kalos rules from battles, and their [[Mega Stone]]s are not banned from trades from Pokémon Omega Ruby and Alpha Sapphire to Pokémon X and Y, despite being otherwise unavailable in those games.
* In the Pokémon X and Y demo, the message "''An immense new power has risen in Mewtwo!''" would be displayed before Mega Evolving. In the final game, the message displayed is "''<Pokémon>'s  is reacting to 's !''" Additionally, Mega Mewtwo Y is referred to as simply Mega Mewtwo.
* According to [[Ken Sugimori]] in his interview in the Nintendo Dream magazine, {{p|Flygon}} was intended to get a Mega Evolution in Pokémon X and Y, but the [[List of unused Pokémon and character designs#Pokémon designs|idea was later dropped]] due to him experiencing an artist's block.[https://www.pokebeach.com/2015/04/mega-flygon-excluded-from-xyoras-because-of-artists-block Mega Flygon Excluded from XY/ORAS Because of Artist's Block | PokeBeach], translation of Nintendo Dream interview scan

===Animated series===
* If not counting {{pkmn|movie}}s or the [[Mega Evolution Specials]], only 37 out of 93 Mega Evolutions have appeared in the {{pkmn|animated series}}.
** Mega {{p|Dragonite}} is the only Mega-Evolved Pokémon to have debuted in the animated series before the official release of the game it first appears.
** Some Mega-Evolved Pokémon introduced in [[Generation VI]] have only debuted in later series:
*** Mega {{p|Steelix}}, Mega {{p|Aggron}}, and Mega {{p|Kangaskhan}} made their animated series debut in {{aniseries|SM}}.
*** Mega {{p|Venusaur}}, Mega {{p|Alakazam}}, Mega {{p|Slowbro}}, Mega {{p|Camerupt}}, and Mega {{p|Altaria}} made their animated series debut in {{aniseries|JN}}.
*** Mega {{p|Sableye}}, Mega {{p|Tyranitar}}, Mega {{p|Medicham}}, Mega {{p|Aerodactyl}}, Mega {{p|Glalie}}, and Mega {{p|Rayquaza}} made their animated series debut in {{aniseries|HZ}}.
* Six Mega-Evolved Pokémon have appeared as {{Shiny}} Pokémon in the animated series, outside of appearances in movies or specials, those being Mega {{p|Metagross}}, Mega {{p|Gyarados}}, Mega {{p|Lucario}}, Mega Lucario Z, Mega Rayquaza, and Mega {{p|Zygarde}}.
** Of those, only Mega Gyarados, Mega Lucario, and Mega Rayquaza have appeared both in their regular and Shiny colorations.
* In {{aniseries|PTS}}, all {{pkmn2|Legendary}} and [[Mythical Pokémon]] that are capable of Mega Evolving can do so without the need of a [[Mega Stone]], [[Key Stone]], or {{pkmn|Trainer}}.
* As of ''Pokémon Horizons: The Series'', the animated series has pitted a Mega-Evolved Pokémon against each of the battle mechanics introduced in subsequent generations.
** In ''[[SM043|When Regions Collide!]]'', {{an|Brock}}'s Mega {{TP|Brock|Steelix}} battled {{an|Kiawe}}'s {{TP|Kiawe|Turtonator}} with its [[Z-Move]], {{m|Inferno Overdrive}} and {{an|Misty}}'s Mega {{TP|Misty|Gyarados}} battled {{Ash}}'s {{TP|Ash|Pikachu}} with its Z-Move, {{m|Gigavolt Havoc}}.
** In ''[[JN086|A Battle of Mega Versus Max]]'', Ash's Mega {{AP|Lucario}} battled [[Bea]]'s [[Gigantamax]] {{p|Machamp}}.
** In ''[[JN116|Pride of a Champion!]]'', [[Diantha]]'s Mega {{TP|Diantha|Gardevoir}} battled {{an|Lance}}'s [[Dynamax]] {{TP|Lance|Dragonite}}.
** In ''[[HZ101|Terastallization vs. Mega Evolution!]]'', [[Roy]]'s Mega {{TP|Roy|Lucario}} battled {{an|Nemona}}'s {{type|Fighting}} {{Tera}} {{p|Pawmot}}.

